text:
Goals
By the end of this lesson you should be able to:

write down the Rasch model and say what \(\theta\) and \(b\) mean;
read an item characteristic curve and a Wright map;
explain why the scale has no natural origin (and what software does about it);
explain why the sum score is all the Rasch model needs to know about a person, and what “specific objectivity” asks of the data;
fit the model to real item response data with mirt, and read item fit statistics and plots to judge where it fits and where it doesn’t.

Goals
By the end of this lesson you should be able to:

write down the common factor model and explain how it reproduces a correlation matrix;
decide how many factors to extract, using eigenvalues and parallel analysis, and explain why common rules of thumb disagree;
explain what rotation does (and what it does not do) and choose between orthogonal and oblique rotations;
say why Likert items call for polychoric correlations, and how factor analysis differs from principal components;
run an exploratory factor analysis on real item responses and read the output.

Core ideas

A model for correlations
The data for factor analysis is a correlation matrix. The common factor model says that each standardized item \(x_i\) is a weighted combination of a small number of common factors plus something unique to that item: \[x_i = \lambda_{i1} f_1 + \lambda_{i2} f_2 + \dots + \lambda_{im} f_m + u_i.\] The weights \(\lambda\) are the loadings. The unique part \(u_i\) bundles measurement error with anything specific to the item, and it is assumed to be uncorrelated with the factors and with the other items’ unique parts. That last assumption carries the model: it says that everything the items have in common runs through the factors. Spearman’s version had one factor; Thurstone (1931) extended it to several, and his Multiple-factor analysis (Thurstone, 1947; see the review by Cattell, 1948) set out most of what follows.
With a single factor, the model makes a strong prediction. The correlation between items \(i\) and \(j\) is just the product of their loadings, \[\text{cor}(x_i, x_j) = \lambda_i \lambda_j,\] so an entire matrix of correlations is generated by one loading per item. Try it.

Try it: correlations from one factor


Code
viewof f1_l = Inputs.form([
  Inputs.range([0, 0.95], {value: 0.8, step: 0.05, label: "Loading, item 1"}),
  Inputs.range([0, 0.95], {value: 0.7, step: 0.05, label: "Loading, item 2"}),
  Inputs.range([0, 0.95], {value: 0.5, step: 0.05, label: "Loading, item 3"}),
  Inputs.range([0, 0.95], {value: 0.3, step: 0.05, label: "Loading, item 4"})
])









Code
f1_cells = f1_l.flatMap((li, i) =&gt; f1_l.map((lj, j) =&gt; ({row: `Item ${i + 1}`, col: `Item ${j + 1}`, r: i === j ? 1 : li * lj})))
Plot.plot({
  width: 420, height: 380, padding: 0.04, x: {label: null, axis: "top"}, y: {label: null},
  color: {type: "linear", domain: [0, 1], range: [palette.white, palette.main]},
  marks: [Plot.cell(f1_cells, {x: "col", y: "row", fill: "r"}),
          Plot.text(f1_cells, {x: "col", y: "row", text: d =&gt; d.r.toFixed(2), fontSize: 14, fill: d =&gt; d.r &gt; 0.6 ? palette.white : palette.ink})]
})
















You should see two things. The item with the weakest loading has the weakest correlations with everything. And the pattern is rigid: the ratio of item 1’s correlations with items 3 and 4 is the same as the ratio of item 2’s. If real data didn’t have that rigid pattern, one factor would not be enough, which is how factor analysis detects that a second dimension is needed. Spearman turned the rigid pattern into a test, the vanishing “tetrad differences” (Spearman & Holzinger, 1924; problem 1).


Code
quiz({
  q: "In a one-factor model, item A has loading 0.8 and item B has loading 0.5. What correlation between A and B does the model imply?",
  options: [
    {text: "0.40", correct: true, why: "One factor means the correlation is the product of the loadings: $0.8 \\times 0.5 = 0.4$."},
    {text: "0.65, the average of the loadings", correct: false, why: "Loadings multiply, they don't average. Each item carries only part of the factor's signal, and a correlation needs both."},
    {text: "It can't be determined without the factor's variance", correct: false, why: "With standardized items and a standardized factor, the loadings are all you need."}
  ]
})







A useful quantity falls out of this: an item’s communality, \(h_i^2 = \sum_k \lambda_{ik}^2\), is the share of its variance explained by the common factors. The remainder, \(1 - h_i^2\), is its uniqueness. An item with loading 0.3 on a single factor has a communality of \(0.3^2 = 0.09\); more than 90% of what it does is its own business.
A one-factor model is what “the items are unidimensional” means in factor-analytic terms, and it is a claim the data can reject.






Recall from Classical test theory and reliability: alpha is not a measure of unidimensionality, and it equals reliability only when the items are tau-equivalent. In the language of this lesson, tau-equivalence is a one-factor model with equal loadings (problem 2).






How many factors?
This is the question exploratory factor analysis is most often used to answer, and it has no single correct procedure. The usual starting point is the eigenvalues of the correlation matrix. Loosely, each eigenvalue says how much of the total variance (which, for \(p\) standardized items, is \(p\)) a dimension can account for, taken in decreasing order. A few large eigenvalues followed by a flat tail suggests a few factors.
Two classic rules of thumb read this list. The Kaiser rule keeps every factor with an eigenvalue above 1 (Guttman, 1954; Kaiser, 1960). The scree plot looks for the “elbow” where the eigenvalues level off (Cattell, 1966). Both have a problem: even data with no factor structure produce eigenvalues above 1, purely from sampling variability, and more so in small samples. Parallel analysis (Horn, 1965) fixes this by comparing the observed eigenvalues with those from random data of the same size, and keeping factors only while the observed ones are larger. Among the simple methods, it is the one I would reach for first.

Try it: eigenvalues and parallel analysis
Twelve items, generated from a known number of factors.


Code
viewof pa_k = Inputs.range([1, 3], {value: 2, step: 1, label: "True number of factors"})
viewof pa_l = Inputs.range([0.2, 0.8], {value: 0.5, step: 0.05, label: "Loading of each item"})
viewof pa_n = Inputs.range([50, 1000], {value: 150, step: 50, label: "Number of people"})

























Code
pa = {
  const p = 12, r = rng(21), sim = (loaded) =&gt; Array.from({length: pa_n}, () =&gt; {
    const f = Array.from({length: pa_k}, () =&gt; r.norm());
    return Array.from({length: p}, (_, j) =&gt; loaded ? pa_l * f[j % pa_k] + Math.sqrt(1 - pa_l ** 2) * r.norm() : r.norm());
  });
  const obs = eigenSym(corMatrix(sim(true)));
  const reps = 15, rand = Array(p).fill(0);
  for (let t = 0; t &lt; reps; t++) eigenSym(corMatrix(sim(false))).forEach((v, i) =&gt; rand[i] += v / reps);
  let nPA = 0; while (nPA &lt; p && obs[nPA] &gt; rand[nPA]) nPA++;
  return {obs, rand, nPA, nKaiser: obs.filter(v =&gt; v &gt; 1).length};
}
Plot.plot({
  width: 700, height: 300, x: {label: "Factor number", ticks: 12}, y: {label: "Eigenvalue"},
  color: {legend: true, domain: ["Your data", "Random data (average)"], range: [palette.main, palette.contrast]},
  marks: [Plot.ruleY([1], {stroke: palette.guide, strokeDasharray: "4 3"}),
          Plot.line(pa.obs.map((v, i) =&gt; ({i: i + 1, v, s: "Your data"})), {x: "i", y: "v", stroke: "s", marker: "circle"}),
          Plot.line(pa.rand.map((v, i) =&gt; ({i: i + 1, v, s: "Random data (average)"})), {x: "i", y: "v", stroke: "s", marker: "circle"})]
})


















Code
md`True number of factors: **${pa_k}**. Parallel analysis suggests **${pa.nPA}**; the eigenvalue-greater-than-one rule suggests **${pa.nKaiser}**.`







At the starting settings (two factors, loadings of 0.5, 150 people), parallel analysis finds two factors and the Kaiser rule four. Raise the sample to 500 and both find two. Now drop the loadings to 0.3: the Kaiser rule says four or five even with 1,000 people, and with 100 people parallel analysis goes wrong too, in either direction depending on the number of factors. The number of factors is an estimate, with its own uncertainty, and the choice should also make substantive sense.


Code
quiz({
  q: "With 100 respondents and 20 items that have no common factors at all, the largest eigenvalue of their correlation matrix will be about…",
  options: [
    {text: "1, since each standardized item has variance 1", correct: false, why: "That is the *average* eigenvalue. Sampling noise spreads them out, so the largest is well above 1."},
    {text: "Well above 1 (a little under 2)", correct: true, why: "Random correlations in a finite sample push the leading eigenvalues above 1 (in 200 simulated datasets of this size, the largest eigenvalue had a median of 1.9). That is why the Kaiser rule over-extracts, and why parallel analysis compares against random data instead."},
    {text: "0, since there is no common factor", correct: false, why: "Eigenvalues of a correlation matrix are never all zero; they always sum to the number of items."}
  ]
})










Rotation
Once we have more than one factor, which set of loadings is the right one? The data cannot say. With two or more factors the loadings are not unique: any rotation of the factors reproduces the correlation matrix exactly as well as any other. What rotation does is pick, from this infinite family of equally good solutions, one that is easy to interpret, ideally one with simple structure (Thurstone, 1947), where each item loads strongly on one factor and near zero on the rest.

Try it: rotating two factors
Six items, with the true loadings shown when the angle is 0. Rotate the factors and watch the loadings change while the fit does not.


Code
viewof rot_a = Inputs.range([-90, 90], {value: 35, step: 1, label: "Rotation angle (degrees)"})









Code
rot = {
  const L0 = [[0.8, 0.1], [0.7, 0.0], [0.6, 0.15], [0.1, 0.75], [0.0, 0.7], [0.2, 0.6]];
  const a = rot_a * Math.PI / 180, c = Math.cos(a), s = Math.sin(a);
  const L = L0.map(([x, y]) =&gt; [c * x - s * y, s * x + c * y]);
  const impl = M =&gt; M.map(r =&gt; M.map(q =&gt; r[0] * q[0] + r[1] * q[1]));
  const d = Math.max(...impl(L).flat().map((v, i) =&gt; Math.abs(v - impl(L0).flat()[i])));
  const simple = L.reduce((u, [x, y]) =&gt; u + Math.abs(Math.abs(x) - Math.abs(y)), 0) / L.length;
  return {L, d, simple};
}
Plot.plot({
  width: 420, height: 420, x: {label: "Loading on factor 1", domain: [-1, 1]}, y: {label: "Loading on factor 2", domain: [-1, 1]},
  marks: [Plot.ruleX([0]), Plot.ruleY([0]),
          Plot.dot(rot.L.map(([x, y], i) =&gt; ({x, y, item: `${i + 1}`, g: i &lt; 3 ? "Items 1–3" : "Items 4–6"})), {x: "x", y: "y", r: 7, fill: "g"}),
          Plot.text(rot.L.map(([x, y], i) =&gt; ({x, y, t: `${i + 1}`})), {x: "x", y: "y", text: "t", dy: -12})],
  color: {legend: true, range: [palette.main, palette.contrast]}
})


















Code
md`Largest change in any implied correlation: **${rot.d.toExponential(1)}** (i.e., none). Simplicity of the loadings (average gap between each item's two loadings): **${rot.simple.toFixed(2)}**${Math.abs(rot_a) &lt; 3 ? " — near its best: each item loads on one factor." : "."}`







The points swing around the origin as a rigid body, and the implied correlations never change. Only the simplicity score moves, and it is largest near an angle of 0, where each item sits close to one axis.






NoteGo deeper: why the data can’t choose a rotation





Assumptions: the common factor model with \(m \ge 2\) factors, loadings in a \(p \times m\) matrix \(\Lambda\), factor correlations \(\Phi\) (the identity for uncorrelated factors), and unique variances in a diagonal matrix \(\Psi\).
The implied correlations. Stacking the model for all \(p\) items gives \(x = \Lambda f + u\), and so \[\text{cor}(x) = \Lambda \Phi \Lambda^\top + \Psi.\]
Orthogonal rotation. Let \(T\) be any orthogonal \(m \times m\) matrix (\(T T^\top = I\)), and take new loadings \(\Lambda^* = \Lambda T\) with \(\Phi = I\). Then \[\Lambda^* \Lambda^{*\top} = \Lambda T T^\top \Lambda^\top = \Lambda \Lambda^\top,\] so every implied correlation is unchanged. The widget’s \(T\) is the \(2 \times 2\) rotation by the slider’s angle.
Oblique rotation. Let \(T\) be any invertible matrix, and take \(\Lambda^* = \Lambda (T^{\top})^{-1}\) and \(\Phi^* = T^\top \Phi T\). Then \(\Lambda^* \Phi^* \Lambda^{*\top} = \Lambda (T^{\top})^{-1} T^\top \Phi T\, T^{-1} \Lambda^\top = \Lambda \Phi \Lambda^\top\). (With the factor variances held at 1, \(T\) must keep the diagonal of \(\Phi^*\) at 1.)
What it means. The likelihood, the residuals and every fit statistic depend on the data only through the implied correlations, so they cannot prefer one \(T\) over another. A rotation criterion (varimax, oblimin, …) is an extra rule for choosing \(T\), and it is a choice about description. The same indeterminacy reappears when a confirmatory model has to fix loadings to be identified (Factor analysis II) and in multidimensional item response models (Dimensionality and multidimensional IRT). If you’ve done the Rasch lesson, it is a cousin of the scale’s missing origin: the data fix differences, not the frame they are described in.



Rotations come in two kinds (Browne, 2001, reviews both). Orthogonal rotations keep the factors uncorrelated; varimax (Kaiser, 1958) is the best known. Oblique rotations let them correlate: oblimin (in R’s default form, direct quartimin; Jennrich & Sampson, 1966), promax (Hendrickson & White, 1964) and others. In psychology and education, constructs tend to correlate with one another, so forcing factors to be uncorrelated is an assumption we seldom have reason to make. My default is oblique. If the factors turn out to be nearly uncorrelated, the two approaches will agree anyway, and we’ll see below how far from zero the BFI-2’s factor correlations sit.


Code
quiz({
  q: "An analyst reports that a varimax solution 'fits better' than an oblimin solution with the same number of factors. What should you make of that?",
  options: [
    {text: "Varimax is the better choice", correct: false, why: "Rotation cannot change fit. With the same number of factors and the same estimation method, all rotations reproduce the correlations identically."},
    {text: "Something is off: rotations of the same solution fit identically", correct: true, why: "Different rotations are different descriptions of the same fitted model. A fit difference means something else changed (the number of factors, the estimator, or the data)."},
    {text: "Oblique rotations always fit better, so the report is backwards", correct: false, why: "Neither fits better. Oblique rotations may be *easier to interpret* when constructs correlate, but the fit is the same."}
  ]
})










Two practical notes
Likert items are not continuous. Pearson correlations between 5-point items understate the relationship between the underlying continuous tendencies, and they are distorted when items are skewed. The standard fix is to factor-analyze polychoric correlations (Olsson, 1979), which estimate the correlation between the continuous variables that the ordered categories are assumed to coarsen. We use them below. This idea, an ordered response as a coarsened continuous one, is also the bridge to item response theory; see Factor analysis II.
Factor analysis is not principal components analysis. PCA (Pearson, 1901; Hotelling, 1933) finds weighted sums of the items that capture as much of their total variance as possible; it has no unique parts and no model of measurement error. Factor analysis models only the shared variance. For summarizing data the two often look similar (Velicer & Jackson, 1990); for asking what constructs an instrument measures, factor analysis is the right tool (Fabrigar et al., 1999). Problem 6 compares the two on real data.

Goals
By the end of this lesson you should be able to:

write down the classical model and define reliability in terms of it;
explain why the correlation between two parallel forms estimates reliability;
compute Cronbach’s alpha, and say why it is a lower bound on reliability (and when it is exact);
use split halves and the Spearman–Brown formula;
run a classical item analysis and use it to spot items that don’t belong, including items that need to be reverse-keyed.

Goals
By the end of this lesson you should be able to:

write down the likelihood for a set of independent observations and say what is fixed and what varies;
explain why we work with the log likelihood, and read a likelihood surface;
connect the curvature of the log likelihood to the standard error of an estimate;
derive the log likelihood of a logistic regression and its derivative, and find the maximum with optim() and with glm();
fit logistic regressions to item responses in long format, and explain why giving items their own intercepts changes the slope.

Core ideas

The likelihood
Suppose we flip a coin 10 times and see 7 heads. What is the probability of heads, \(p\)? We don’t know, but we can ask a sharper question: for each candidate value of \(p\), how probable is the sequence we actually observed? For independent flips that probability is a product, \[L(p \mid y) = \prod_{i=1}^{n} p^{y_i}(1-p)^{1-y_i} = p^{k}(1-p)^{n-k},\] where \(y_i = 1\) for heads and \(k\) is the number of heads. Read as a function of the data, this is a probability. Read as a function of \(p\) with the data held fixed, it is the likelihood (Fisher, 1922). Nothing about the data changes as we move along it; we are asking which values of the parameter the data favour.
Note that the likelihood is not a probability distribution for \(p\): it needn’t integrate to 1. In the frequentist reading, \(p\) is fixed but unknown, and the likelihood ranks candidate values. In a Bayesian reading, \(p\) gets a prior distribution, and the likelihood is what turns the prior into a posterior. We use the first reading here and return to priors in Estimating abilities.

Try it: the likelihood for a coin


Code
viewof cf_n = Inputs.range([1, 200], {value: 10, step: 1, label: "Flips (n)"})
viewof cf_k0 = Inputs.range([0, 200], {value: 7, step: 1, label: "Heads (k)"})
viewof cf_log = Inputs.toggle({label: "Show the log likelihood", value: false})

























Code
cf_k = Math.min(cf_k0, cf_n)
cf_data = grid(0.001, 0.999, 400).map(p =&gt; {
  const ll = cf_k * Math.log(p) + (cf_n - cf_k) * Math.log(1 - p);
  return {p, ll, L: Math.exp(ll)};
})
cf_max = cf_data.reduce((a, b) =&gt; (b.ll &gt; a.ll ? b : a))
Plot.plot({
  width: 700, height: 300,
  x: {label: "Candidate p", domain: [0, 1]},
  y: {label: cf_log ? "Log likelihood" : "Likelihood", ...(cf_log ? {domain: [cf_max.ll - 25, cf_max.ll + 1]} : {})},
  marks: [
    Plot.ruleX([cf_k / cf_n], {stroke: palette.guide, strokeDasharray: "4,3"}),
    Plot.line(cf_data.filter(d =&gt; !cf_log || d.ll &gt; cf_max.ll - 25), {x: "p", y: cf_log ? "ll" : "L", stroke: palette.main, strokeWidth: 2.5})
  ]
})
































Code
md`With ${cf_k} heads in ${cf_n} flips, the likelihood peaks at p = ${(cf_k / cf_n).toFixed(2)}, the proportion of heads. Its height there is ${cf_max.L.toExponential(2)}.`







Move the sliders and note two things. The peak always sits at \(k/n\): the proportion of heads is the maximum likelihood estimate (MLE). And the height of the peak collapses as \(n\) grows. With 200 flips even the most probable sequence is wildly improbable, because any particular sequence of 200 flips is. The height means nothing on its own; the shape is what we use.


Code
quiz({
  q: "In the likelihood $L(p \\mid y)$, what is held fixed and what varies?",
  options: [
    {text: "The data are fixed; the parameter $p$ varies", correct: true, why: "We observed one set of flips. The likelihood asks how probable that set is under each candidate $p$."},
    {text: "The parameter is fixed; the data vary", correct: false, why: "That is the probability model read forwards: for a known $p$, how probable is each possible dataset? The likelihood reads it backwards."},
    {text: "Both vary", correct: false, why: "If both varied we'd be back to a joint probability. The likelihood fixes what we saw."}
  ]
})










Work on the log scale
Products of many probabilities are tiny numbers, small enough that a computer’s arithmetic eventually loses them: the likelihood of a few thousand flips is smaller than the smallest number a double-precision float can hold. So in practice we work with the log likelihood, \[\ell(p) = \log L(p) = k\log p + (n-k)\log(1-p).\] The log turns the product into a sum, which is also far easier to differentiate. And because the log is monotone, whatever maximizes \(\ell\) also maximizes \(L\). Turn on the log likelihood in the widget above: the peak doesn’t move.


The likelihood surface and its curvature
How sure should we be about the estimate? The likelihood answers that too. Here are log likelihoods for the same proportion of heads from three sample sizes, each shifted so its peak sits at zero.

Try it: more data, sharper peak


Code
viewof cv_phat = Inputs.range([0.1, 0.9], {value: 0.7, step: 0.05, label: "Proportion of heads"})









Code
cv_ns = [20, 200, 2000]
cv_data = cv_ns.flatMap(n =&gt; grid(0.01, 0.99, 400).map(p =&gt; ({
  p, n: `n = ${n}`,
  rel: n * (cv_phat * Math.log(p / cv_phat) + (1 - cv_phat) * Math.log((1 - p) / (1 - cv_phat)))
}))).filter(d =&gt; d.rel &gt; -6)
Plot.plot({
  width: 700, height: 300,
  x: {label: "Candidate p", domain: [0, 1]}, y: {label: "Log likelihood (peak at 0)", domain: [-6, 0.2]},
  color: {legend: true, domain: cv_ns.map(n =&gt; `n = ${n}`), range: [palette.light, palette.main, palette.contrast]},
  marks: [
    Plot.ruleY([-2], {stroke: palette.rule}),
    Plot.line(cv_data, {x: "p", y: "rel", stroke: "n", strokeWidth: 2.5})
  ]
})

























Code
Inputs.table(cv_ns.map(n =&gt; ({n, "standard error of p̂": Math.sqrt(cv_phat * (1 - cv_phat) / n).toFixed(3)})), {layout: "auto"})







The peak is in the same place each time, but with more data it is narrower: the log likelihood falls away faster as we move from the estimate, so fewer values of \(p\) remain plausible. That curvature is the precision of the estimate. For the coin, the second derivative of \(\ell\) at the peak is \(-n/(\hat p(1-\hat p))\), and one over the square root of its negative is the familiar standard error, \(\sqrt{\hat p(1-\hat p)/n}\), shown in the table. This link between curvature and precision runs through the whole course: it becomes information in Information, precision, and short forms and the standard error of an ability estimate in Estimating abilities.


Code
quiz({
  q: "You flip the coin four times as often and see the same proportion of heads. What happens to the standard error of $\\hat p$?",
  options: [
    {text: "It halves", correct: true, why: "The curvature of the log likelihood grows in proportion to $n$, so the standard error, one over its square root, shrinks by $\\sqrt{4} = 2$."},
    {text: "It falls to a quarter", correct: false, why: "The curvature quadruples, but the standard error goes with its square root."},
    {text: "It stays the same, because the proportion is the same", correct: false, why: "The estimate stays the same; its precision doesn't. More flips give a sharper peak."}
  ]
})










Logistic regression by likelihood
Now let the probability depend on a predictor. Recall logistic regression (we write its coefficients as \(\beta\)s, keeping \(b\) for item difficulty from the Rasch model on): for a binary outcome \(y_i\) and a predictor \(x_i\), \[p_i = \Pr(y_i = 1 \mid x_i) = \frac{\exp(\beta_0 + \beta_1 x_i)}{1 + \exp(\beta_0 + \beta_1 x_i)}.\] Each observation has its own \(p_i\), but the likelihood is built exactly as for the coin: \[\ell(\beta_0, \beta_1) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \Big[\, y_i \log p_i + (1 - y_i)\log(1 - p_i) \,\Big].\] We know the \(y_i\) and \(x_i\); the \(p_i\) depend on the unknown \(\beta_0\) and \(\beta_1\). Differentiating (problem 1 asks you to) gives a pleasingly simple result: \[\frac{\partial \ell}{\partial \beta_1} = \sum_i (y_i - p_i)\,x_i, \qquad \frac{\partial \ell}{\partial \beta_0} = \sum_i (y_i - p_i).\] At the maximum both derivatives are zero. The second equation merits emphasis: at the MLE, the model’s predicted number of successes equals the observed number.

Try it: fit a logistic regression by hand
Sixty simulated observations. Move the intercept and slope to make the curve fit the points, and watch the log likelihood and the two derivatives.


Code
lg_sample = {
  const r = rng(7);
  return Array.from({length: 60}, () =&gt; {
    const x = r.norm();
    return {x, y: r.unif() &lt; logistic(-0.3 + 1.2 * x) ? 1 : 0};
  });
}
lg_ll = (b0, b1) =&gt; lg_sample.reduce((s, d) =&gt; {
  const p = logistic(b0 + b1 * d.x);
  return s + (d.y ? Math.log(p) : Math.log(1 - p));
}, 0)
lg_mle = {
  let b0 = 0, b1 = 0; // Newton-Raphson
  for (let it = 0; it &lt; 30; it++) {
    let g0 = 0, g1 = 0, h00 = 0, h01 = 0, h11 = 0;
    for (const d of lg_sample) {
      const p = logistic(b0 + b1 * d.x), w = p * (1 - p);
      g0 += d.y - p; g1 += (d.y - p) * d.x;
      h00 += w; h01 += w * d.x; h11 += w * d.x * d.x;
    }
    const det = h00 * h11 - h01 * h01;
    b0 += (h11 * g0 - h01 * g1) / det;
    b1 += (h00 * g1 - h01 * g0) / det;
  }
  return {b0, b1};
}
viewof lg_b0 = Inputs.range([-2, 2], {value: 0, step: 0.05, label: "Intercept β0"})
viewof lg_b1 = Inputs.range([-1, 3], {value: 0, step: 0.05, label: "Slope β1"})







































Code
Plot.plot({
  width: 700, height: 280, x: {label: "x"}, y: {label: "y, and P(y = 1)", domain: [-0.08, 1.08]},
  marks: [
    Plot.dot(lg_sample, {x: "x", y: (d, i) =&gt; d.y + ((i * 37) % 11 - 5) * 0.012, r: 3, fill: palette.guide}),
    Plot.line(grid(-3, 3).map(x =&gt; ({x, p: logistic(lg_b0 + lg_b1 * x)})), {x: "x", y: "p", stroke: palette.main, strokeWidth: 2.5})
  ]
})









Code
